  "text": " The coordinates of the center of the circumscribed sphere, denoted as \\( C(x_c, y_c, z_c) \\), can be expressed as the midpoint of the cube since the sphere passes through all vertices of the cube, which are \\((\\pm 1, \\pm 1, \\pm 1)\\) in this case.\n\nLet's find the coordinates step by step.\n\nThe equation of the sphere is given by,\n\n\\[ (x - x_c)^2 + (y - y_c)^2 + (z - z_c)^2 = R^2 \\]\n\nWhere \\( (x_c, y_c, z_c) \\) are the coordinates of the center of the sphere, and \\( R \\) is the radius of the sphere. Since the points \\((\\pm 1, \\pm 1, \\pm 1)\\) lie on the surface of the sphere, we can substitute these points into the equation of the sphere. \n\nTherefore,\n\n\\[ (1 - x_c)^2 + (1 - y_c)^2 + (1 - z_c)^2 = R^2 \\]\n\\[ (-1 - x_c)^2 + (-1 - y_c)^2 + (-1 - z_c)^2 = R^2 \\]\n\nDifferentiating the above equations with respect to \\(x\\), \\(y\\), and \\(z\\), we obtain the tangency conditions:\n\n\\[ 2(1 - x_c) = 0 \\]\n\\[ 2(1 - y_c) = 0 \\]\n\\[ 2(1 - z_c) = 0 \\]\n\nSolving these equations, we get,\n\n\\[ x_c = 1 \\]\n\\[ y_c = 1 \\]\n\\[ z_c = 1 \\]\n\nSo, we have the coordinates of the center of the circumscribed sphere, \\( C(x_c, y_c, z_c) \\equiv C(1, 1, 1) \\).\n\nTo prove that the sphere is tangent to the axes at the points where they intersect the cube, let's check this by substituting \\(x = 1\\), \\(y = 1\\), and \\(z = 1\\) into the equation of the sphere.\n\n\\[ (1 - 1)^2 + (1 - 1)^2 + (1 - 1)^2 = R^2 \\]\n\\[ 0 + 0 + 0 = R^2 \\]\n\\[ R^2 = 0 \\]\n\\[ R = 0 \\]\n\nThis implies that the sphere touches the axes at points where the axes intersect the cube. Since the cube has vertices at \\((\\pm 1, \\pm 1, \\pm 1)\\), the points where the axes intersect the cube are \\((\\pm 1, \\pm 1, 0)\\), \\((\\pm 1, 0, \\pm 1)\\), and \\((0, \\pm 1, \\pm 1)\\). Each of these points lies on the axes, confirming the tangency.\n\nGiven the logic and step-by-step reasoning above, we conclude that the center of the circumscribed sphere \\( C \\) has the coordinates \\( (1, 1, 1) \\).\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{(1, 1, 1)} \\]\n\n.symmetric\n.symmetric\n\n.symmetric",
